SUMMARY:Haunted Hallows at Mashpee Commons
DTSTART:20261009T000000
DTEND:20261031T000000
DTSTAMP:20261029T174705Z
UID:95ffc8651422542480c590df868a02@massachusetts-events
DESCRIPTION:For generations\, the Lord of Morrisey Manor kept to his own h
 alls. This October\, he escaped\, and he has taken up residence in the Hau
 nted Hallows at Mashpee Commons! Come if you dare! Haunted Hallows is a 30
 -minute immersive Halloween experience built on one simple idea: you contr
 ol the fear. When you arrive\, you choose your scare level. Dial it down t
 o a little boo. Dial it up to extreme shock. Same haunt\, same story\, sam
 e Lord but the night is yours to set. That means the whole family can walk
  in together and everyone gets exactly the night they came for. Open every
  Friday and Saturday October 9-31. Buy tickets now! From 6:15 PM: Early Re
 leases The first releases of the night\, while there's still light in the 
 sky. Physically gentler and suitable for kids of all ages: no total darkne
 ss\, no chase\, no contact. You get the story\, the sets\, and a very thea
 trical Lord and his very odd household. Through the 8:45 PM release: After
  Dark Once the light goes\, the Hallows change. Groups are still released 
 one at a time at your booked time\, so you go through with your own people
  and never stand in a line\, but this is where the full haunt lives. Later
  releases are limited and do sell out. Before you face the Lord\, come wan
 der Halloweentown\, set up right at the Mashpee Farmers Market and free to
  walk through. No Hallows ticket required. Opens 5:00 PM at the Farmers Ma
 rket on the Village Green at Mashpee Commons. Brought to you by the Mashpe
 e Chamber of Commerce to support the Mashpee Improvement Fund.\n\nSources:
  Cape Cod Chamber\nEvent page: https://www.capecodchamber.org/event/haunte
 d-hallows-at-mashpee-commons/52435/
LOCATION:Mashpee Commons\, 4 Job's Fishing Road\, Mashpee\, MA\, 02649
URL:https://www.capecodchamber.org/event/haunted-hallows-at-mashpee-common
 s/52435/
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:The Precious Things We Share: The Past\, Present\, and Future of I
 ndigenous Picture Book Art
DTSTART:20261009T000000
DTEND:20270620T000000
DTSTAMP:20261029T174705Z
UID:38854f507a5d9c365a359a9be2bb1e@massachusetts-events
DESCRIPTION:Wáanegugish means “the precious things we share” in the N
 ipmuc language—a fitting title for this exhibition\, the first of its ki
 nd celebrating and centering Indigenous picture book artists from what is 
 now the United States and Canada. Exploring the illustrative traditions of
  the Peoples of Turtle Island and spanning more than 250 years of storytel
 ling\, it highlights the growth of children’s books created by Indigenou
 s artists and the vibrant stories and images that reflect Native lives\, c
 ultures\, and traditions. The exhibition includes more than 80 works in fo
 ur sections. Since Time Immemorial explains how many North American Indige
 nous peoples created elaborate and dynamic stories through material cultur
 e prior to European contact and colonization\, by exhibiting artwork and o
 bjects made by contemporary artists who reinterpret and honor traditions o
 f craft. In Early Books \, visitors can see first editions of books by Nat
 ive artists and authors from the early twentieth century\, and The Rise of
  Illustrative Natives explores contemporary publishing. The Seventh Genera
 tion elevates the voices of up-and-coming Native artists and showcases art
 work of the stories that inform the next generation of readers. Guest-cura
 ted by Dr. Lee Francis IV (Laguna Pueblo) and co-curated by Eleanor Owl St
 olzoff (Eastern Band of Cherokee Indians) working alongside an Advisory Gr
 oup of Native community leaders\, artists\, publishers\, philanthropists\,
  educators\, and writers. The exhibition is made possible in part by the I
 nstitute of Museum and Library Services (Federal grant award ID: MA-256047
 -OMS-24) and Mass Humanities through the Massachusetts Cultural Council.\n
 \nSources: Meet Boston\nEvent page: https://www.meetboston.com/event/the-p
 recious-things-we-share%3a-the-past-present-and-future-of-indigenous-pictu
 re-book-art/93682/
LOCATION:The Eric Carle Museum of Picture Book Art\, 125 W. Bay Road\, Amh
 erst\, MA\, 01002
URL:https://www.meetboston.com/event/the-precious-things-we-share%3a-the-p
 ast-present-and-future-of-indigenous-picture-book-art/93682/
END:VEVENT
