BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:Newburyport Art presents May Exhibitions at 65 Water Street
DTSTART:20261114T110000
DTEND:20261114T170000
DTSTAMP:20261029T174705Z
UID:724d0b32839c3d0025f908383c1118@massachusetts-events
DESCRIPTION:Newburyport Art (NAA) presents three new shows opening in the 
 month of May! Soft Structures is a group exhibition that explores fibers a
 nd fibrous material as process and metaphor. This exhibit features over 80
  artworks in a variety of media including drawing\, painting\, sculpture\,
  weaving\, embroidery\, quilting\, collage\, mixed media and more. This ex
 hibit is on view in the first-floor Sargent Gallery and second-floor Harts
 on Gallery. FOLIA is a collaborative exhibition featuring works by Amesbur
 y artists Amy Vander Els & Heather Karp. This exhibit explores the quiet b
 eauty and joyful rhythm of leaves and the symbolic meaning behind many tha
 t speaks of perseverance\, resilience and hope. Thirty-six artworks are di
 splayed amongst spring greenery in the first-floor Hills Gallery. Amy and 
 Heather will give a free artist talk on Thursday\, May 21st from 6:00-7:00
 pm\, concluding with Q&A. The 11th Annual Outdoor Sculpture Garden Exhibit
 ion debuted on May 1st\, and will remain on view year-round until April 20
 27. At nearly 3\,000 square feet\, the Range Lights Sculpture Garden resid
 es behind the historic NAA and is seen and visited by hundreds of people e
 very week. This year’s exhibition features 16 pieces by 10 local and reg
 ional artists. The participating artists include\; Jeffrey Briggs\, Lindle
 y Briggs\, Sarah Burns and Mike Platt\, Alexis Guieb-Alimbuyuguen\, Andrew
  Henschel\, Mae Kenny\, Evan Ralph\, Michael Updike and Raynor Warner. A r
 eception for FOLIA and Soft Structures will be held on Saturday\, May 2nd 
 from 4-6pm\, with the 11th Annual Sculpture Exhibit reception beginning at
  3:00pm\, including a Best in Show award at 3:30pm. The receptions are fre
 e and open to the public\, and visitors participating in the May 2nd Newbu
 ryport Artwalk are encouraged to stop by. New Hours: Wednesday through Sun
 day from 11:00am–5:00pm. The gallery is handicap accessible. More inform
 ation\, as well as an online gallery is available at newburyportart.org. F
 ounded in 1948\, Newburyport Art\, a 501(c)(3) non-profit\, is dedicated t
 o promoting and exhibiting members’ work and to providing art education 
 and access to the visual arts for the entire community. The gallery and ev
 ents are free and open to the public.\n\nSources: North of Boston\nEvent p
 age: https://northofboston.org/event/newburyport-art-presents-may-exhibiti
 ons-at-65-water-street/2026-11-14/
LOCATION:Newburyport Art Association\, 65 Water St\, Newburyport\, MA\, 01
 950
URL:https://northofboston.org/event/newburyport-art-presents-may-exhibitio
 ns-at-65-water-street/2026-11-14/
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:Noguchi and Japanese American Internment During World War II
DTSTART:20261114T130000
DTEND:20261114T150000
DTSTAMP:20261029T174705Z
UID:60dbef6247e8dfd14d6e78f1e94889@massachusetts-events
DESCRIPTION:Join curator and scholar Christina Hiromi Hobbs for a talk exa
 mining Isamu Noguchi’s voluntary incarceration at the Poston War Relocat
 ion Center in Arizona during World War II. Hobbs will discuss Noguchi’s 
 complex motivations for entering the camp\, discuss its impact on his life
  and work and examine the historical context of art making during Japanese
  American incarceration. After the bombing of Pearl Harbor in 1941\, more 
 than 120\,000 people of Japanese ancestry were incarcerated in internment 
 camps created on U.S. soil. Many lived there for the duration of the war. 
 Read more about their stories — and why their experiences still matter t
 oday — from the Densho documentation project and Poston Preservation.\n
 \nSources: North of Boston\nEvent page: https://northofboston.org/event/no
 guchi-and-japanese-american-internment-during-world-war-ii/
LOCATION:Peabody Essex Museum\, East India Square\, 161 Essex Street\, Sal
 em\, MA\, 01970
URL:https://northofboston.org/event/noguchi-and-japanese-american-internme
 nt-during-world-war-ii/
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:Remembrance Altars
DTSTART:20261114T103000
DTEND:20261114T120000
DTSTAMP:20261029T174705Z
UID:e23bb213324bd972c333dfc5e1b059@massachusetts-events
DESCRIPTION:From the ofrendas of Dia de los Muertos to butsudan in Japanes
 e Buddhist homes\, many different cultures create spaces for honoring ance
 stors. As the days grow shorter\, join us for a reflective art workshop th
 at reflects on those who came before us. Through simple mixed-media assemb
 lage\, participants will create a personal altar or remembrance piece that
  celebrates family\, chosen family\, mentors\, cultural heritage or loved 
 ones who have shaped their lives. No prior art experience is needed. All m
 aterials will be provided\, and participants are invited to bring copies o
 f photographs or small paper mementos they may wish to incorporate into th
 eir artwork. Studio Saturdays is a series of adult art making classes desi
 gned to inspire creativity and connection through multimedia arts explorat
 ion. Each class includes an arts activity that will inspire collaboration
 \, curiosity and creativity\, culminating in a completed individual artwor
 k and communal sharing.\n\nSources: North of Boston\nEvent page: https://n
 orthofboston.org/event/remembrance-altars/
LOCATION:Peabody Essex Museum\, East India Square\, 161 Essex Street\, Sal
 em\, MA\, 01970
URL:https://northofboston.org/event/remembrance-altars/
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:Schulenburg Preview Reception
DTSTART:20261114T000000
DTEND:20261114T000000
DTSTAMP:20261029T174705Z
UID:d2ae9006f235f8a67ae1570777e360@massachusetts-events
DESCRIPTION:Please join us for a gallery preview of selected works schedul
 ed for Paul Schulenburg's solo exhibition at the St. Botolph Club in Bosto
 n starting in December. The works going to Boston may be previewed here ht
 tps://addisonart.com/schulenburg-stbotolphClub/ If there are any pieces yo
 u would particularly like to see before they go to Boston\, please let us 
 know and we will bring them into the gallery. The St. Botolph Club\, locat
 ed at 199 Commonwealth Avenue\, was founded in 1880. It promoted the new a
 rt of its time\, and sponsored John Singer Sargent's first one-man show in
  America in 1888. Works by Claude Monet were displayed in 1892\, 1895\, an
 d 1899. By 1900\, the Club had sponsored solo exhibitions of virtually eve
 ry important American Impressionist of the era including Childe Hassam\, T
 heodore Wendel\, John L. Breck\, Edmund Tarbell\, and Mary Cassatt. We hop
 e you can join us in celebrating this prestigious show.\n\nSources: Cape C
 od Chamber\nEvent page: https://www.capecodchamber.org/event/schulenburg-p
 review-reception/52681/
LOCATION:Addison Art Gallery\, 43 South Orleans Road (Route 28)\, Orleans
 \, MA\, 02653
URL:https://www.capecodchamber.org/event/schulenburg-preview-reception/526
 81/
END:VEVENT
