BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:Newburyport Art presents May Exhibitions at 65 Water Street
DTSTART:20261119T110000
DTEND:20261119T170000
DTSTAMP:20261029T174705Z
UID:654845ccf3447b026a4e73cda6ab45@massachusetts-events
DESCRIPTION:Newburyport Art (NAA) presents three new shows opening in the 
 month of May! Soft Structures is a group exhibition that explores fibers a
 nd fibrous material as process and metaphor. This exhibit features over 80
  artworks in a variety of media including drawing\, painting\, sculpture\,
  weaving\, embroidery\, quilting\, collage\, mixed media and more. This ex
 hibit is on view in the first-floor Sargent Gallery and second-floor Harts
 on Gallery. FOLIA is a collaborative exhibition featuring works by Amesbur
 y artists Amy Vander Els & Heather Karp. This exhibit explores the quiet b
 eauty and joyful rhythm of leaves and the symbolic meaning behind many tha
 t speaks of perseverance\, resilience and hope. Thirty-six artworks are di
 splayed amongst spring greenery in the first-floor Hills Gallery. Amy and 
 Heather will give a free artist talk on Thursday\, May 21st from 6:00-7:00
 pm\, concluding with Q&A. The 11th Annual Outdoor Sculpture Garden Exhibit
 ion debuted on May 1st\, and will remain on view year-round until April 20
 27. At nearly 3\,000 square feet\, the Range Lights Sculpture Garden resid
 es behind the historic NAA and is seen and visited by hundreds of people e
 very week. This year’s exhibition features 16 pieces by 10 local and reg
 ional artists. The participating artists include\; Jeffrey Briggs\, Lindle
 y Briggs\, Sarah Burns and Mike Platt\, Alexis Guieb-Alimbuyuguen\, Andrew
  Henschel\, Mae Kenny\, Evan Ralph\, Michael Updike and Raynor Warner. A r
 eception for FOLIA and Soft Structures will be held on Saturday\, May 2nd 
 from 4-6pm\, with the 11th Annual Sculpture Exhibit reception beginning at
  3:00pm\, including a Best in Show award at 3:30pm. The receptions are fre
 e and open to the public\, and visitors participating in the May 2nd Newbu
 ryport Artwalk are encouraged to stop by. New Hours: Wednesday through Sun
 day from 11:00am–5:00pm. The gallery is handicap accessible. More inform
 ation\, as well as an online gallery is available at newburyportart.org. F
 ounded in 1948\, Newburyport Art\, a 501(c)(3) non-profit\, is dedicated t
 o promoting and exhibiting members’ work and to providing art education 
 and access to the visual arts for the entire community. The gallery and ev
 ents are free and open to the public.\n\nSources: North of Boston\nEvent p
 age: https://northofboston.org/event/newburyport-art-presents-may-exhibiti
 ons-at-65-water-street/2026-11-19/
LOCATION:Newburyport Art Association\, 65 Water St\, Newburyport\, MA\, 01
 950
URL:https://northofboston.org/event/newburyport-art-presents-may-exhibitio
 ns-at-65-water-street/2026-11-19/
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:“To the Farthest Ports of the Rich East” Lecture
DTSTART:20261119T183000
DTEND:20261119T200000
DTSTAMP:20261029T174705Z
UID:6c0a63e447bbfc7c9506eeab117836@massachusetts-events
DESCRIPTION:Join James Fichter as he discusses profit and peril in the new
  trade to Asia With independence from Britain secured\, in the 1780s Ameri
 can began trading to Asia for themselves. They bought tea in China. They s
 ailed to the East India Company-administered India for cloth\, to Yemen an
 d Dutch Java for coffee\, to Sumatra for pepper\, and to many other ports 
 in between. The trade to Asia accelerated as the French Revolutionary and 
 Napoleonic Wars (1793-1815) kept European traders out of Asian waters\, cr
 eating a tussle between between British and American crews to supply Frenc
 h-occupied Europe with Asian goods. This process created a new class of Am
 erican investor-capitalists and the first American millionaires. Yet such 
 wealth came at a cost\, as the ever growing and increasingly wealthy Ameri
 can trading fleet was a tempting target for foreign states and pirates—f
 rom Britain and France to Barbary and Yemen—against which the U.S. Navy 
 was not always a match.\n\nSources: North of Boston\nEvent page: https://n
 orthofboston.org/event/to-the-farthest-ports-of-the-rich-east-lecture/
LOCATION:Salem Armory Visitor Center\, 2 New Liberty Street\, Salem\, MA\,
  01970
URL:https://northofboston.org/event/to-the-farthest-ports-of-the-rich-east
 -lecture/
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:UNEARTHING JOY IN CRITICAL LITERACY DISCUSSIONS
DTSTART:20261119T000000
DTEND:20261119T000000
DTSTAMP:20261029T174705Z
UID:0b93b18608cd91305911aca2b6f84c@massachusetts-events
DESCRIPTION:In this free professional development seminar\, Peter Piazza\,
  Ph.D.\, will discuss strategies for critical literacy instruction\, as dr
 awn from Gholdy Muhammad’s “Unearthing Joy.” A key text in Stonehill
 ’s Justice & Anti-Discrimination in Education program\, “Unearthing Jo
 y” guides teachers through Muhammad’s framework for moving beyond our 
 narrow focus on rote learning and test-based measurement. This virtual sem
 inar will highlight key strategies for building students’ critical refle
 ction skills and\, indeed\, returning joy to learning. After discussing th
 e framework\, participants will look at an example lesson plan developed b
 y a Stonehill graduate student based on the “Unearthing Joy” model.\n
 \nSources: Cape Cod Chamber\nEvent page: https://www.capecodchamber.org/ev
 ent/unearthing-joy-in-critical-literacy-discussions/52565/
LOCATION:Stonehill College Division of Graduate and Professional Studies\,
  320 Washington Street Stonehill College\, NORTH EASTON\, MA\, 02356
URL:https://www.capecodchamber.org/event/unearthing-joy-in-critical-litera
 cy-discussions/52565/
END:VEVENT
